          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Call your local emergency number now, or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait and do not try to manage this at home.**\n\nBeing able to say only a few words before needing air, and no relief from your rescue inhaler, is a sign of severe breathing difficulty. The reference material says this needs immediate emergency attention (*Asthma*, condition-asthma; *Severe difficulty breathing*, red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty). The automated symptom check returned a \"routine\" tier. That does not match the guidance in those same documents, so please disregard it. Do not wait for it to get worse.\n\nWhile you wait for help:\n- Sit upright. This is usually the easiest position for breathing.\n- Loosen tight clothing and try to stay calm, since anxiety makes breathing harder (*Severe difficulty breathing*).\n- If you have a written action plan from your clinician, follow it. Do not delay calling for help.\n\nTell the emergency team straight away if you notice blue or grey lips or fingertips, confusion, drowsiness, or chest pain. These are also emergency signs. If someone is with you, ask them to call for help now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
